Let a phone's Vulkan device exist when its driver refuses the feature chain.

Halide asks for exactly what vkGetPhysicalDeviceFeatures reported, which every desktop driver
honours. Adreno 660 (Snapdragon 888, OnePlus 9) does not: vkCreateDevice returns an error as
soon as the Vulkan 1.2 8-bit storage struct is in the pNext chain, whatever its fields say, so
the context never exists, copy_to_device fails, and the engine has no developer on the phone at
all. The device is now created three ways: as asked, then with the chain cut to 16-bit storage,
then asking for nothing. A kernel that needs a feature it did not get fails when it runs, which
the host reports; a device that never exists develops nothing and says only that it is missing.

diff --git a/src/runtime/vulkan_context.h b/src/runtime/vulkan_context.h
--- a/src/runtime/vulkan_context.h
+++ b/src/runtime/vulkan_context.h
@@ -444,6 +444,20 @@
 
     VkResult result = vkCreateDevice(*physical_device, &device_create_info, alloc_callbacks, device);
     if (result != VK_SUCCESS) {
+        // A mobile driver can answer a feature query and then refuse the same bits back in a
+        // device. Adreno 660 declines the chain outright once the 1.2 8-bit storage struct rides
+        // in it, every field false or not. Ask again with the chain cut back to 16-bit storage,
+        // then asking for nothing at all, and let a kernel that misses a feature say so when it
+        // runs: the host can report that, where a device that never exists develops nothing.
+        storage_16bit_ext.pNext = nullptr;
+        result = vkCreateDevice(*physical_device, &device_create_info, alloc_callbacks, device);
+    }
+    if (result != VK_SUCCESS) {
+        device_create_info.pNext = nullptr;
+        device_create_info.pEnabledFeatures = nullptr;
+        result = vkCreateDevice(*physical_device, &device_create_info, alloc_callbacks, device);
+    }
+    if (result != VK_SUCCESS) {
         debug(user_context) << "Vulkan: vkCreateDevice failed with return code: " << vk_get_error_name(result) << "\n";
         return halide_error_code_device_interface_no_device;
     }
